  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions continue along with above-normal\ntemperatures through Saturday.\n\nAs an upper-level ridge across the Tennessee Valley builds \neastward through the remainder of this week, it will continue to\ndirect sprawling surface high pressure over the region. This \nsurface high however, will eventually become more centered to \nour south by late week and offshore by the start of the \nweekend.\n\nDry conditions and a mainly clear sky is expected to continue\nthrough Saturday. This will be accompanied by above-normal\ntemperatures with the warmth peaking on Friday as some areas \ntop out near 70 degrees (some 10-15 degrees above average for \nthis time of year). It will still be chilly at night, with lows \nmainly in the 30s to low 40s. A rather dry airmass looks to \nremain in place due to the upper-level ridge and surface high, \nhowever the surface dew points should start to increase by \nSaturday as some moisture begins to increase as a light onshore \nflow becomes more established. \n\nA few instances of patchy shallow ground fog are possible over \nthe coastal plain the next couple of nights, however should be \nrather insignificant.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Confidence continues to increase in a coastal low\ndeveloping off the Southeast US, potentially bringing impacts \nto our region Sunday into Monday.\n\nForecaster confidence continues to gradually uptick regarding a\ndeepening area of low pressure developing off the Southeast \nCoast late week and into the weekend. As the axis of the upper \nlevel ridge shifts offshore, some broad troughing is expected to\nreside over the Gulf Coast region. With energy then arriving \nfrom the west, a closed low is expected to develop Saturday off \nthe north Florida coast.\n\nWhat occurs beyond Saturday though is quite uncertain, however \nforecast guidance is becoming better aligned up to this point. \nAlmost all guidance (including many major forecast models along\nwith associated AI/ensemble members) have a strengthening area \nof low pressure developing over the Gulf Stream/northern Bahamas \nwhile slowly tracking northward; somewhere near/along the \nCarolina coast by Sunday. The upper level pattern then differs \nwith regard to timing and strength of an approaching upper \ntrough across the central CONUS and how quickly the western \nperiphery of a surface high over the North Atlantic recedes. \n\nWhile it is too early for specifics of what impacts may be \nbrought to the region, this bears watching as this could be the \nnext chance for some beneficial rainfall. While PoPs are still \ncurrently around 30-50% (highest near the coast), this may be \non the conservative side considering latest guidance that has \narrived this morning.",
